    "poster_id": 1991,
    "post_rendered": "We consider two sequences of real numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/e/18ee4674af3e4412c95dd5917d3b907f2f850cd3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_{1} \\geq x_{2} \\geq \\ldots \\geq x_{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"148\" height=\"14\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/d/a4d6145c89c0c0e3cceba3bd555c61b182ec3d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\ y_{1} \\geq y_{2} \\geq \\ldots \\geq y_{n}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"153\" height=\"15\" > Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/e/e2eee2e9a668b3fcbf4188b9ad413482e6d8be28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z_{1}, z_{2}, .\\ldots, z_{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"105\" height=\"11\" > be a permutation of the numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/6/1066b7070ca9fd16038a213bfb2a974595ef00b8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y_{1}, y_{2}, \\ldots, y_{n}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"102\" height=\"11\" > Prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/1/6f16d74415561e5b892427f593a9db0188115b7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum \\limits_{i=1}^{n} ( x_{i} -\\ y_{i} )^{2} \\leq \\sum \\limits_{i=1}^{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"154\" height=\"48\" > <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/3/bd3411a8490c1b924a0391c4118b4472e5368235.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$( x_{i} - z_{i})^{2}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"19\" >",
    "post_canonical": "We consider two sequences of real numbers $x_{1} \\geq x_{2} \\geq \\ldots \\geq x_{n}$ and $\\ y_{1} \\geq y_{2} \\geq \\ldots \\geq y_{n}.$ Let $z_{1}, z_{2}, .\\ldots, z_{n}$ be a permutation of the numbers $y_{1}, y_{2}, \\ldots, y_{n}.$ Prove that $\\sum \\limits_{i=1}^{n} ( x_{i} -\\ y_{i} )^{2} \\leq \\sum \\limits_{i=1}^{n}$ $( x_{i} - z_{i})^{2}.$",
