   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 367455,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 61047,
    "poster_id": 1991,
    "post_rendered": "Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/9/8995c8a8615365138fa5b9580f3e4c32dfed1202.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{1}, \\ldots, a_{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"11\" > be an infinite sequence of strictly positive integers, so that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/5/be553a81f82afb818e32c595ae8fe760defad78e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{k} &lt; a_{k+1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"14\" > for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/4/5d48e0968a64d782fe75bca620ce749938b7d0bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k.$\"  width=\"13\" height=\"12\" > Prove that there exists an infinity of terms <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/7/c47e4f53298e874644937c24d74d6a7fea1a624f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ a_{m},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"26\" height=\"11\" > which can be written like <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/5/065104105e3b0d66b7d6de092dc9f16ca1b99324.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_m = x \\cdot a_p + y \\cdot a_q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"147\" height=\"15\" > with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/4/b6400c6fe3f1ed70e5afd387a9686cdc3fa09531.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x,y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"27\" height=\"11\" > strictly positive integers and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/e/ede949777e586be8360fde4aa94dd90c57925317.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p \\neq q.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"17\" >",
    "post_canonical": "Let $a_{1}, \\ldots, a_{n}$ be an infinite sequence of strictly positive integers, so that $a_{k} < a_{k+1}$ for any $k.$ Prove that there exists an infinity of terms $ a_{m},$ which can be written like $a_m = x \\cdot a_p + y \\cdot a_q$ with $x,y$ strictly positive integers and $p \\neq q.$",
    "username": "orl",
